---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import ParticleWidget from "../components/ParticleWidget.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="What causes an outbreak — and what stops one? — COMP4020/8020 Assignment 1"
  description="An interactive, from-scratch particle simulation exploring what makes an outbreak explode and what makes it end: contact rate, isolation, masks, vaccination, and community structure."
>
  <h1>What causes an outbreak to explode — and what makes it end?</h1>
  <p class="lede">One bouncing dot in a room of 149 others, rebuilt from scratch as a particle simulation — scroll through it to see what turns a single case into a sweep through the whole room, and what turns the same outbreak back off.</p>

  <section class="panel" id="simple-case">
    <h2><span class="section-icon" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40" width="32" height="32" fill="none" stroke="#94a3b8" stroke-width="2"><circle cx="8" cy="10" r="3"></circle><circle cx="20" cy="8" r="3"></circle><circle cx="32" cy="12" r="3"></circle><circle cx="10" cy="24" r="3"></circle><circle cx="30" cy="26" r="3"></circle><circle cx="12" cy="34" r="3"></circle><circle cx="28" cy="34" r="3"></circle><circle cx="20" cy="22" r="3" fill="#fb7159" stroke="#fb7159"></circle></svg></span> One infected person, in a room of 149</h2>
    <p class="explainer">Every dot below is a person, moving at random inside a closed room. One of them, shown in orange, starts infected. Nobody is trying to avoid anyone else — there are no rules yet, just motion and proximity. Watch what happens before we measure anything.</p>
    <p class="explainer">Three states are all this model needs: Susceptible (blue, can still catch it), Infected (orange, contagious right now), and Recovered (grey, immune and no longer spreading it).</p>
    <ParticleWidget
      id="simple"
      mode="simple"
      showChart={false}
      sliders={["betaGamma"]}
      betaDefault={0.35}
      gammaDefault={0.1}
      ariaLabel="Simple case: one infected particle bouncing among 149 others"
    />
    <p class="explainer">Two dials drive everything here: β (beta), how readily the virus jumps from an infected person to a susceptible one, and γ (gamma), how fast an infected person recovers. Push β up or γ down and the room fills with orange faster; the formula above — R0 = β / γ — is the single number that tells you whether an outbreak grows or dies out, before you've even run a frame.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel" id="measuring">
    <h2><span class="section-icon" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40" width="32" height="32" fill="none" stroke-width="2"><rect x="2" y="14" width="10" height="12" rx="2" stroke="#38bdf8"></rect><rect x="15" y="14" width="10" height="12" rx="2" stroke="#fb7159"></rect><rect x="28" y="14" width="10" height="12" rx="2" stroke="#94a3b8"></rect><path d="M12 20 H15 M25 20 H28" stroke="#5b5f6b"></path></svg></span> How do we read an outbreak's chart?</h2>
    <p class="explainer">The chart below tracks what share of the room is in each state, moment by moment, from the instant the run starts — day zero stays anchored on the left no matter how long you leave it running. Watch the blue susceptible band shrink as the orange infected band rises, peaks, and gives way to grey.</p>
    <ParticleWidget
      id="measuring"
      mode="simple"
      sliders={["betaGamma"]}
      betaDefault={0.4}
      gammaDefault={0.08}
      ariaLabel="Measuring an outbreak: the same simple case, now paired with a live S/I/R chart"
    />
    <p class="explainer">Same two dials as the last section, tuned a little more aggressively so the chart's whole shape — rise, peak, decline — plays out in view rather than crawling.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel" id="virus-module">
    <h2><span class="section-icon" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40" width="32" height="32" fill="none" stroke="#fb7159" stroke-width="2"><circle cx="20" cy="20" r="9"></circle><circle cx="20" cy="20" r="2.4" fill="#fb7159" stroke="none"></circle><line x1="20" y1="6" x2="20" y2="11"></line><line x1="20" y1="29" x2="20" y2="34"></line><line x1="6" y1="20" x2="11" y2="20"></line><line x1="29" y1="20" x2="34" y2="20"></line><line x1="10" y1="10" x2="13.5" y2="13.5"></line><line x1="30" y1="30" x2="26.5" y2="26.5"></line><line x1="30" y1="10" x2="26.5" y2="13.5"></line><line x1="10" y1="30" x2="13.5" y2="26.5"></line></svg></span> Big module 1 — what the virus itself brings to the table</h2>
    <p class="explainer">Every outbreak is a tug-of-war between two different kinds of numbers: what the virus itself is capable of, and how people behave around it. Start with the virus — nothing in this module is about behaviour yet, just what happens the instant two bodies get close.</p>

    <div class="panel-sub" id="radius">
      <h3><span class="section-icon" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40" width="28" height="28" fill="none" stroke="#fb7159" stroke-width="2"><circle cx="20" cy="20" r="14" stroke-dasharray="4 3"></circle><circle cx="20" cy="20" r="3" fill="#fb7159" stroke="none"></circle></svg></span> Infection needs proximity</h3>
      <p class="explainer">Two people only risk transmission when they're within some distance of each other. The halo below traces that boundary live, around every infected dot — anyone drifting inside it is close enough to be at risk this frame.</p>
      <ParticleWidget id="radius" mode="simple" sliders={["radius"]} ariaLabel="Infection radius demo: a visible halo around every infected particle" />
    </div>

    <div class="panel-sub" id="infectious-period">
      <h3><span class="section-icon" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40" width="28" height="28" fill="none" stroke="#fb7159" stroke-width="2"><circle cx="20" cy="20" r="15"></circle><path d="M20 11 V20 L27 25"></path></svg></span> How long someone stays contagious</h3>
      <p class="explainer">The other virus property is duration: how many days someone stays contagious before recovering. A longer infectious period means more chances to pass it on before that clock runs out, even with nothing else about the disease changed.</p>
      <ParticleWidget id="period" mode="simple" sliders={["infectiousPeriod"]} ariaLabel="Infectious period demo: adjust how many days an infection lasts" />
    </div>

    <div class="panel-sub" id="transmission-chance">
      <h3><span class="section-icon" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40" width="28" height="28" fill="none" stroke="#fb7159" stroke-width="2"><path d="M6 28 A14 14 0 0 1 34 28"></path><line x1="20" y1="28" x2="28" y2="16"></line><circle cx="20" cy="28" r="2" fill="#fb7159"></circle></svg></span> Chance of infection per contact</h3>
      <p class="explainer">The last virus property is transmission chance: given a contact within range, how likely is it that the virus actually jumps across? Real diseases differ wildly here — it's the number the disease track further down the page is built from.</p>
      <ParticleWidget id="chance" mode="simple" sliders={["chance"]} chanceDefault={15} ariaLabel="Chance of infection demo: adjust how likely a contact is to transmit" />
      <p class="explainer">In the real world, radius, duration, and chance combine into what epidemiologists call the basic reproduction number, R0 — and it isn't a fixed property of a virus. It moves with contact rate and population density: the same pathogen behaves differently in a sparse suburb than in a packed dormitory (CDC, <em>Emerging Infectious Diseases</em>, <a href="https://wwwnc.cdc.gov/eid/article/25/1/17-1901_article">"Complexity of the Basic Reproduction Number (R0)," 2019</a>).</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="panel" id="activity-module">
    <h2><span class="section-icon" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40" width="32" height="32" fill="none" stroke="#38bdf8" stroke-width="2"><circle cx="14" cy="12" r="4"></circle><path d="M6 30 C6 22 10 19 14 19 C18 19 22 22 22 30"></path><circle cx="29" cy="15" r="3.4"></circle><path d="M22 30 C22 24 25.5 21.5 29 21.5 C32.5 21.5 36 24 36 30"></path></svg></span> Big module 2 — what people do about it</h2>
    <p class="explainer">The virus's own numbers only tell half the story. What people actually do — whether the sick isolate, whether errands cross communities, whether they mask up or get vaccinated first — changes the outcome just as much, sometimes more.</p>

    <div class="panel-sub" id="isolation">
      <h3><span class="section-icon" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40" width="28" height="28" fill="none" stroke="#38bdf8" stroke-width="2"><path d="M20 4 L34 10 V20 C34 29 28 34 20 37 C12 34 6 29 6 20 V10 Z"></path><path d="M14 20 L18 24 L27 14" stroke="#0f9d58"></path></svg></span> Does isolating the sick actually help?</h3>
      <p class="explainer">Once someone tests positive, isolating them stops every contact they would otherwise have made. In this simulation that isolation is a real, visible place — a fenced-off corner of the room, not just a rule enforced off-screen. Below are two copies of the exact same starting scenario — same room, same random seed — one with isolation switched on, the other without. Nothing else differs; only the outcome does.</p>
      <div class="rec-pair">
        <div>
          <ParticleWidget id="isoa" mode="simple" badge="Isolation off" ariaLabel="Isolation comparison: no isolation" />
        </div>
        <div>
          <ParticleWidget id="isob" mode="simple" badge="Isolation on" ariaLabel="Isolation comparison: infected cases isolated" />
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="btn-row">
        <button class="btn" id="isolation-reset-both" type="button">Reset both, same seed</button>
      </div>
      <p class="explainer">Isolation alone has a blind spot: it only removes people once they're already known to be sick, and by then they may have spent days silently contagious. Combined contact tracing and testing needs to reach roughly 60% efficacy before it reliably pushes the reproduction number below the tipping point (<a href="https://publichealth.jmir.org/2021/10/e32468/">JMIR Public Health and Surveillance, 2021</a>).</p>
    </div>

    <div class="panel-sub" id="hotspot">
      <h3><span class="section-icon" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40" width="28" height="28" fill="none" stroke="#fb7159" stroke-width="2"><circle cx="20" cy="20" r="3" fill="#fb7159"></circle><circle cx="6" cy="8" r="2.5"></circle><circle cx="34" cy="8" r="2.5"></circle><circle cx="6" cy="32" r="2.5"></circle><circle cx="34" cy="32" r="2.5"></circle><line x1="20" y1="20" x2="6" y2="8"></line><line x1="20" y1="20" x2="34" y2="8"></line><line x1="20" y1="20" x2="6" y2="32"></line><line x1="20" y1="20" x2="34" y2="32"></line></svg></span> One shared errand can undo the rest</h3>
      <p class="explainer">Switch the layout and almost nothing else changes: everyone still has their own home base and wanders locally most of the time. The only difference is a small, constant chance that any one person makes a trip to a single shared, central location — a grocery store, a train platform — before heading home. Watch how much faster the outbreak moves, even though individual behaviour barely changed. (This "one shared errand" framing is the idea popularised by 3Blue1Brown's 2020 video, <em>Simulating an Epidemic</em>.) Drag β and γ here too: the same tipping point applies, just with everyone occasionally routed through one shared spot instead of only bumping into neighbours.</p>
      <ParticleWidget
        id="hotspot"
        mode="central"
        sliders={["betaGamma"]}
        betaDefault={0.25}
        gammaDefault={0.12}
        ariaLabel="Central location case: everyone occasionally passes through one shared point"
      />
    </div>

    <div class="panel-sub" id="communities">
      <h3><span class="section-icon" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40" width="28" height="28" fill="none" stroke="#94a3b8" stroke-width="2"><line x1="20" y1="2" x2="20" y2="38"></line><line x1="2" y1="20" x2="38" y2="20"></line><circle cx="10" cy="10" r="2.5" fill="#38bdf8" stroke="none"></circle><circle cx="30" cy="10" r="2.5" fill="#38bdf8" stroke="none"></circle><circle cx="10" cy="30" r="2.5" fill="#38bdf8" stroke="none"></circle><circle cx="30" cy="30" r="2.5" fill="#fb7159" stroke="none"></circle></svg></span> Splitting into separate communities helps</h3>
      <p class="explainer">Now the same room is split into four separate communities — the lines below aren't decoration, they're the boundaries. People spend nearly all their time inside their own community and bounce off its walls just like in the simple case; only a small, fixed chance each frame sends someone travelling to a different one.</p>
      <ParticleWidget id="communities" mode="communities" ariaLabel="Communities case: four separate groups with rare travel between them" />
      <p class="explainer">Compare this against the simple case above at similar settings: splitting a population into loosely-connected groups caps how far a single outbreak can spread, even when no individual person is behaving any differently — the structure of who can reach whom matters as much as anyone's individual choices.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="panel-sub" id="travel">
      <h3><span class="section-icon" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40" width="28" height="28" fill="none" stroke="#94a3b8" stroke-width="2"><line x1="20" y1="2" x2="20" y2="38" stroke-dasharray="3 3"></line><line x1="2" y1="20" x2="38" y2="20" stroke-dasharray="3 3"></line><path d="M11 11 C18 8 24 24 30 30" stroke="#fb7159" fill="none"></path><circle cx="30" cy="30" r="2.5" fill="#fb7159" stroke="none"></circle></svg></span> Communities aren't perfectly sealed</h3>
      <p class="explainer">Real communities leak: people commute, visit family, go on holiday. This slider controls exactly one thing — the chance, each frame, that someone currently in one community relocates to a random other one. Turn it up and watch the four-community advantage from above erode as the boundaries stop mattering.</p>
      <ParticleWidget id="travel" mode="communities" sliders={["travel"]} travelDefault={0.1} ariaLabel="Inter-community travel demo: adjust how often people relocate between communities" />
    </div>

    <div class="panel-sub" id="masks">
      <h3><span class="section-icon" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40" width="28" height="28" fill="none" stroke="#38bdf8" stroke-width="2"><circle cx="20" cy="20" r="12"></circle><path d="M11 22 C14 26 26 26 29 22" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="2.5"></path></svg></span> Masks cut the odds, not the contact</h3>
      <p class="explainer">Masks don't stop people from meeting — they cut the odds that any one meeting actually passes the virus on. Slide the percentage of the room wearing one; masked people show a thin white ring around their dot so you can spot them moving through the crowd.</p>
      <ParticleWidget id="masks" mode="simple" sliders={["mask"]} maskDefault={50} ariaLabel="Mask-wearing demo: adjust what share of the room wears a mask" />
    </div>

    <div class="panel-sub" id="vaccination">
      <h3><span class="section-icon" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40" width="28" height="28" fill="none" stroke="#0f9d58" stroke-width="2"><line x1="12" y1="28" x2="26" y2="14"></line><line x1="17" y1="19" x2="23" y2="25"></line><circle cx="28" cy="12" r="3" fill="#0f9d58" stroke="none"></circle></svg></span> Vaccination starts the room ahead</h3>
      <p class="explainer">Vaccination works differently again: instead of changing what happens during a contact, it removes people from the susceptible pool before the simulation even starts. Slide the starting vaccination rate and watch how many dots begin the run already grey — recovered without ever having been sick.</p>
      <ParticleWidget id="vaccination" mode="simple" sliders={["vaccination"]} vaccinationDefault={30} ariaLabel="Vaccination demo: adjust what share of the room starts immune" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="panel" id="real-diseases">
    <h2><span class="section-icon" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40" width="32" height="32" fill="none" stroke="#f59e0b" stroke-width="2"><line x1="4" y1="20" x2="36" y2="20"></line><line x1="4" y1="14" x2="4" y2="26"></line><line x1="14" y1="16" x2="14" y2="24"></line><line x1="24" y1="16" x2="24" y2="24"></line><line x1="36" y1="14" x2="36" y2="26"></line><circle cx="20" cy="20" r="3" fill="#f59e0b"></circle></svg></span> Where real diseases sit on this scale</h2>
    <p class="explainer">This simulation's sliders aren't calibrated to any real disease's actual reproduction number — but the diseases below are real, and their R0 estimates come straight from the published literature, placed on a single logarithmic axis from R0 = 1 to R0 = 20 so wildly different diseases can share one line without their labels colliding. Press a marker to preview a similarly-ranked intensity in the sandbox further down; the shape of the outbreak is the point of the comparison, not an exact translation of the number.</p>

    <div class="r0-track">
      <div class="r0-track-line"></div>
      <button class="r0-marker r0-marker-above" style="left: 8%" type="button" data-disease-preset="flu" data-toggle="flu-detail" data-toggle-group="diseases" aria-expanded="false">
        <span class="r0-marker-label">Flu · 1.3</span>
        <span class="r0-marker-dot"></span>
      </button>
      <button class="r0-marker r0-marker-below" style="left: 21%" type="button" data-disease-preset="ebola" data-toggle="ebola-detail" data-toggle-group="diseases" aria-expanded="false">
        <span class="r0-marker-dot"></span>
        <span class="r0-marker-label">Ebola · 1.5–2.2</span>
      </button>
      <button class="r0-marker r0-marker-above" style="left: 33%" type="button" data-disease-preset="covid" data-toggle="covid-detail" data-toggle-group="diseases" aria-expanded="false">
        <span class="r0-marker-label">COVID-19 · 2.7</span>
        <span class="r0-marker-dot"></span>
      </button>
      <button class="r0-marker r0-marker-below" style="left: 90%" type="button" data-disease-preset="measles" data-toggle="measles-detail" data-toggle-group="diseases" aria-expanded="false">
        <span class="r0-marker-dot"></span>
        <span class="r0-marker-label">Measles · 12–18</span>
      </button>
    </div>

    <p id="diseases-placeholder" class="disease-detail-placeholder">Press a disease above (or a regime button below) to see its source and estimate.</p>
    <div id="flu-detail" class="disease-detail" hidden>
      <p>Seasonal influenza is estimated around R0 ≈ 1.28, pooled across outbreaks (<a href="https://stacks.cdc.gov/view/cdc/25291/cdc_25291_DS1.pdf">Biggerstaff et al., BMC Infectious Diseases, 2014</a>).</p>
    </div>
    <div id="covid-detail" class="disease-detail" hidden>
      <p>Early COVID-19 estimates pooled to roughly R0 ≈ 2.66, with a plausible range of about 1.4–3.9 depending on setting and method (<a href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9517346/">systematic review, PMC, 2022</a>).</p>
    </div>
    <div id="ebola-detail" class="disease-detail" hidden>
      <p>Ebola outbreaks have been estimated around R0 ≈ 1.5–2.2 depending on the outbreak and region (<a href="https://transportgeography.org/contents/applications/transportation-pandemics/basic-reproduction-number-r0-of-major-infectious-diseases/">Geography of Transport Systems</a>).</p>
    </div>
    <div id="measles-detail" class="disease-detail" hidden>
      <p>Measles is dramatically more transmissible than anything else on this track — R0 ≈ 12–18 in a fully susceptible population, which is why it takes such a high vaccination rate to keep it controlled (<a href="https://stacks.cdc.gov/view/cdc/80426/cdc_80426_DS1.pdf">CDC</a>).</p>
    </div>

    <div class="btn-row">
      <button class="btn" type="button" data-disease-preset="below" data-toggle="below-detail" data-toggle-group="diseases" aria-expanded="false">R0 &lt; 1</button>
      <button class="btn" type="button" data-disease-preset="at" data-toggle="at-detail" data-toggle-group="diseases" aria-expanded="false">R0 ≈ 1</button>
      <button class="btn" type="button" data-disease-preset="above" data-toggle="above-detail" data-toggle-group="diseases" aria-expanded="false">R0 &gt; 1</button>
    </div>
    <div id="below-detail" class="disease-detail" hidden>
      <p>R0 below 1 means each infected person, on average, infects fewer than one other — the outbreak shrinks over time. SARS-CoV in 2003 and COVID-19 during 2020's lockdowns both landed here through deliberate intervention, not luck (<a href="https://wwwnc.cdc.gov/eid/article/10/7/03-0647_article">CDC EID, 2004</a>; <a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-020-2405-7">Flaxman et al., Nature, 2020</a>).</p>
    </div>
    <div id="at-detail" class="disease-detail" hidden>
      <p>R0 sitting right at 1 is the tipping point itself. The herd immunity threshold works out to 1 − 1/R0, so the more transmissible a disease is, the more of the population needs to be immune before it stops spreading on its own (<a href="https://royalsociety.org/-/media/policy/projects/set-c/set-c-herd-immunity.pdf">Royal Society SET-C report, 2020</a>).</p>
    </div>
    <div id="above-detail" class="disease-detail" hidden>
      <p>Above 1, each generation of cases is larger than the last — left alone, that's exponential growth, which is exactly what the sandbox below will show if you push both sliders up.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="panel" id="sandbox">
    <h2><span class="section-icon" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40" width="32" height="32" fill="none" stroke="#38bdf8" stroke-width="2"><line x1="10" y1="6" x2="10" y2="34"></line><line x1="20" y1="6" x2="20" y2="34"></line><line x1="30" y1="6" x2="30" y2="34"></line><circle cx="10" cy="24" r="3" fill="#38bdf8"></circle><circle cx="20" cy="14" r="3" fill="#fb7159"></circle><circle cx="30" cy="20" r="3" fill="#94a3b8"></circle></svg></span> Put it all together</h2>
    <p class="explainer">Every control from this page, in one place: layout, infection radius, chance per contact, infectious period, inter-community travel, mask uptake, starting vaccination rate, and isolation. Press one of the disease markers above and it jumps down here with a similarly-ranked setup already loaded.</p>
    <ParticleWidget
      id="sandbox"
      mode="simple"
      sliders={["radius", "chance", "infectiousPeriod", "travel", "mask", "vaccination"]}
      showQuarantineCheckbox
      showModeTabs
      ariaLabel="Capstone sandbox: full control over layout, every slider, and isolation"
    />
  </section>

  <section class="panel" id="quiz">
    <h2><span class="section-icon" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40" width="32" height="32" fill="none" stroke="#7c3aed" stroke-width="2"><circle cx="20" cy="20" r="16"></circle><path d="M15 15 C15 11 25 11 25 16 C25 19 21 19 21 23"></path><circle cx="21" cy="28" r="1.4" fill="#7c3aed" stroke="none"></circle></svg></span> Quick check</h2>
    <p class="explainer">Four quick questions, built entirely from what's already on this page. Pick an answer — a wrong one just shakes and lets you try again.</p>
    <div class="quiz-grid">
      <div class="quiz-card" data-quiz-answer="a">
        <p class="quiz-question">If R0 = 3, does the outbreak grow or shrink over time?</p>
        <div class="btn-row">
          <button class="btn quiz-choice" type="button" data-quiz-choice="a">Grows</button>
          <button class="btn quiz-choice" type="button" data-quiz-choice="b">Shrinks</button>
        </div>
        <p class="quiz-feedback" aria-live="polite"></p>
        <span class="quiz-mark quiz-mark-correct" aria-hidden="true">✓</span>
        <span class="quiz-mark quiz-mark-wrong" aria-hidden="true">✗</span>
      </div>
      <div class="quiz-card" data-quiz-answer="b">
        <p class="quiz-question">You double γ (people recover twice as fast) and leave β unchanged. Does R0 go up or down?</p>
        <div class="btn-row">
          <button class="btn quiz-choice" type="button" data-quiz-choice="a">Up</button>
          <button class="btn quiz-choice" type="button" data-quiz-choice="b">Down</button>
        </div>
        <p class="quiz-feedback" aria-live="polite"></p>
        <span class="quiz-mark quiz-mark-correct" aria-hidden="true">✓</span>
        <span class="quiz-mark quiz-mark-wrong" aria-hidden="true">✗</span>
      </div>
      <div class="quiz-card" data-quiz-answer="b">
        <p class="quiz-question">You split one room of 150 into four communities with almost no travel between them. Does a single outbreak spread further, or stay more contained?</p>
        <div class="btn-row">
          <button class="btn quiz-choice" type="button" data-quiz-choice="a">Spreads further</button>
          <button class="btn quiz-choice" type="button" data-quiz-choice="b">Stays contained</button>
        </div>
        <p class="quiz-feedback" aria-live="polite"></p>
        <span class="quiz-mark quiz-mark-correct" aria-hidden="true">✓</span>
        <span class="quiz-mark quiz-mark-wrong" aria-hidden="true">✗</span>
      </div>
      <div class="quiz-card" data-quiz-answer="b">
        <p class="quiz-question">Half the room wears masks that cut per-contact infection chance roughly in half. Does the outbreak tend to grow faster or slower than in an unmasked room?</p>
        <div class="btn-row">
          <button class="btn quiz-choice" type="button" data-quiz-choice="a">Faster</button>
          <button class="btn quiz-choice" type="button" data-quiz-choice="b">Slower</button>
        </div>
        <p class="quiz-feedback" aria-live="polite"></p>
        <span class="quiz-mark quiz-mark-correct" aria-hidden="true">✓</span>
