  <section class="panel" id="takeaways">
    <h2><span class="section-icon" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40" width="32" height="32" fill="none" stroke="#0f9d58" stroke-width="2"><circle cx="20" cy="20" r="16"></circle><path d="M13 21 L18 26 L28 14"></path></svg></span> What this toy model actually tells us</h2>
    <p class="explainer">Underneath every widget on this page is the same formal idea: the SIR model. Everyone is Susceptible, Infected, or Recovered, and only two rates govern how people move between those three states — β, the transmission rate, and γ, the recovery rate. Their ratio, R0 = β / γ, is the tipping point: above 1, each generation of cases outnumbers the last and the outbreak grows; below 1, it shrinks toward nothing; at exactly 1, it's balanced on the edge.</p>
    <p class="explainer">Every control on this page is really just a way of pushing β or γ around. Infection radius and chance of infection per contact both raise or lower β directly — more opportunities to transmit, or a higher chance each time one occurs. Infectious period is the inverse of γ: stretch it out, and γ falls, so R0 climbs even if nothing about transmission itself changed. Masks lower the effective β by cutting the odds of each contact. Isolation and the shared-hotspot layout both change how many susceptible people an infected person can actually reach, which is β in disguise. Communities and the travel slider between them change who counts as "in contact" at all — shrinking or reopening the pool β gets to act on. Vaccination doesn't touch β or γ; it starts the clock with fewer susceptible people to infect in the first place, which is why it can flatten an outbreak that a change in β or γ alone couldn't.</p>
    <ul class="explainer">
      <li>A contact rate just above the tipping point is the difference between a handful of cases and a sweep through the whole population.</li>
      <li>Isolating confirmed cases helps, but it can't catch what's still incubating silently — testing and tracing speed matter as much as the isolation itself.</li>
      <li>One shared crowded space can undo a dozen individually-careful decisions.</li>
      <li>Splitting into separate, loosely-connected groups caps how far a single outbreak spreads, even without lowering anyone's individual risk — and a little cross-community travel gives that cap back.</li>
      <li>Masks and vaccination act on the outbreak in two genuinely different ways: one lowers β, the other shrinks the pool β has to work with.</li>
      <li>Real diseases sit at wildly different points on this same R0 scale, and that's why some outbreaks fizzle out and others don't.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="explainer">The particle engine, layout, and every line of prose on this page were written from scratch for this assignment. The three-mode structure (simple / central location / communities) draws on the same genre of interactive explainer as Nicky Case's <em>Explorable Explanations</em> and 3Blue1Brown's <em>Simulating an Epidemic</em> (2020), which popularised this style of visualisation; the real-world figures throughout are drawn from the CDC, the Royal Society, and the peer-reviewed sources linked above. This is an illustrative toy model, not the kind of epidemiological modelling used to plan an actual public-health response — the shapes are the takeaway, not the exact numbers.</p>
  </section>

